The excerpt doesn't include the value of the port constant. It imports `STUDIO_PLUGIN_PORT` from `rbx_studio_server`, so its definition isn't shown.

The code that handles a port that's already in use:

```rust
let listener =
    tokio::net::TcpListener::bind((Ipv4Addr::new(127, 0, 0, 1), STUDIO_PLUGIN_PORT)).await;
```

```rust
} else {
    tracing::info!("This MCP instance will use proxy since port is busy");
    tokio::spawn(async move {
        dud_proxy_loop(server_state_clone, close_rx).await;
    })
};
```

If the bind fails, the program logs that it "will use proxy since port is busy" and starts `dud_proxy_loop`. That function's implementation isn't included, so the excerpt doesn't show how the proxying works.

